          "headersSize": 699,
          "cookies": [],
          "redirectURL": "",
          "content": {
            "mimeType": "application/json",
            "text": "{\"id\":86,\"filmId\":70,\"screeningDate\":\"2026-01-20T20:45\",\"doorsTime\":\"8.15pm\",\"filmTime\":\"8.45\",\"capacity\":37,\"ticketsSold\":37,\"price\":1100,\"format\":\"Digital\",\"excludeFromGiftVouchers\":false,\"film\":{\"id\":70,\"title\":\"LOVE HOTEL\",\"description\":\"In a single night inside a Tokyo love hotel, two damaged lives intersect under the pressure of desperation, fantasy, and self-erasure. A failed businessman planning his own death hires a call girl, only for their transaction to unravel into something unstable and emotionally raw. Love Hotel operates within the framework of pink cinema, yet consistently resists its expectations, redirecting erotic spectacle toward exhaustion, confession, and psychological exposure. Sex here is not titillation but a temporary suspension of consequence—a space where shame can be voiced without permanence.\\n\\nSōmai’s long takes and restless camera movement trap the characters in real time, refusing narrative shortcuts or moral framing. The hotel room becomes a liminal zone between performance and collapse, where power shifts moment to moment and intimacy feels both compulsory and unreachable. Stripped of glamour and redemption, Love Hotel presents desire as a survival mechanism already failing, and connection as something briefly possible only when nothing else remains intact.\",\"runtime\":88,\"year\":1985,\"country\":\"Japan\",\"director\":\"Shinji Sōmai\",\"imageUrl\":\"https://www.siff.net/images/CINEMA/2025/Film%20Jun-Dec/CIN_LoveHotel_1600x900.jpg\",\"imageCropX\":50,\"imageCropY\":50,\"ageCertificate\":\"18\"}}",
