          "headersSize": 697,
          "cookies": [],
          "redirectURL": "",
          "content": {
            "mimeType": "application/json",
            "text": "{\"id\":78,\"filmId\":64,\"screeningDate\":\"2026-01-29T18:30\",\"doorsTime\":\"6pm\",\"filmTime\":\"6.30pm\",\"capacity\":37,\"ticketsSold\":22,\"price\":1100,\"format\":\"Digital\",\"excludeFromGiftVouchers\":false,\"film\":{\"id\":64,\"title\":\"FASCINATION\",\"description\":\"In the isolated countryside outside Paris, a fugitive thief seeks refuge in a remote château, only to find himself ensnared in a ritualistic world governed by enigmatic women and unspoken rules. Fascination distils Jean Rollin’s obsessions into their most austere form: minimal dialogue, heightened gestures, and eroticism drained of warmth. \\n\\nVampirism here is less a narrative engine than a social order—formal, aristocratic, and terminally bored—where violence is ceremonial and desire is rigidly choreographed. Rollin’s camera lingers on bodies and interiors with clinical detachment, emphasising repetition over suspense and atmosphere over causality. The film’s spare structure and sudden brutality converge in a conclusion that rejects romanticism entirely, presenting immortality not as excess or ecstasy, but as a hollow system sustained by habit, blood, and decay.\\n\\n+ Introduction by Filmmaker & Programmer, Craig Williams\",\"runtime\":82,\"year\":1979,\"country\":\"France\",\"director\":\"Jean Rollin\",\"imageUrl\":\"https://player.bfi.org.uk/sites/default/files/hero-images/de75422e2765f34a830f2c7a5fd2f3f8.jpg\",\"imageCropX\":50,\"imageCropY\":50,\"ageCertificate\":\"18\"}}",
