          "headersSize": 697,
          "cookies": [],
          "redirectURL": "",
          "content": {
            "mimeType": "application/json",
            "text": "{\"id\":102,\"filmId\":82,\"screeningDate\":\"2026-02-07T16:00\",\"doorsTime\":\"3.30pm\",\"filmTime\":\"4pm\",\"capacity\":37,\"ticketsSold\":0,\"price\":1100,\"format\":\"Digital\",\"excludeFromGiftVouchers\":false,\"film\":{\"id\":82,\"title\":\"DARK OF THE SUN\",\"description\":\"During the Congo Crisis, a band of mercenaries is contracted to retrieve a cache of high-grade diamonds from deep in rebel territory and escort them back to a besieged port. Led by the hardened and morally conflicted Bruce Curry, the group’s mission fractures under the weight of exhaustion, fear, and competing agendas, forcing brutal choices at every turn. As alliances shift and the landscape becomes increasingly hostile, the expedition transforms from a strategic operation into a visceral struggle for survival.\\n\\nAdapted from Wilbur Smith’s adventure novel, Dark of the Sun exemplifies late-1960s war cinema that eschews conventional heroism in favour of a grittier, more ambiguous outlook. Jack Cardiff’s direction foregrounds the chaos of conflict and the moral murk of extractive violence, situating the narrative within broader critiques of imperial ambition and human frailty.\",\"runtime\":110,\"year\":1968,\"country\":\"USA/UK\",\"director\":\"Jack Cardiff\",\"imageUrl\":\"https://images.mubicdn.net/images/notebook/post_spotlight_images/5532/cache-5532-1647270550/images-w1280.jpg\",\"imageCropX\":0,\"imageCropY\":13,\"ageCertificate\":\"15\"}}",
