          "headersSize": 697,
          "cookies": [],
          "redirectURL": "",
          "content": {
            "mimeType": "application/json",
            "text": "{\"id\":104,\"filmId\":84,\"screeningDate\":\"2026-02-07T20:50\",\"doorsTime\":\"8.20pm\",\"filmTime\":\"8.50pm\",\"capacity\":37,\"ticketsSold\":0,\"price\":1100,\"format\":\"Digital\",\"excludeFromGiftVouchers\":false,\"film\":{\"id\":84,\"title\":\"DEATHDREAM\",\"description\":\"After surviving a near-fatal ambush in Vietnam, Mark returns home to his family — but he’s not quite the same. Though alive in body, he manifests symptoms that disturb his loved ones: a pallid complexion, bullet wounds that won’t heal, a craving for raw meat, and an eerie inability to feel pain. As his behaviour grows increasingly erratic, the boundary between soldier and revenant blurs, forcing his family to confront a presence that seems alive only in the grotesque sense.\\n\\nA brooding fusion of war trauma narrative and supernatural horror, Death Dream — also released under the title Dead of Night — channels the cultural anxieties of the post-Vietnam era into a slow-burn tale of dislocation, guilt, and corporeal unsettlement. Clark’s restrained direction amplifies the inevitability of decay and moral dissolution, making the film a compelling reflection on loss, reintegration, and the psychological residues of violence.\",\"runtime\":87,\"year\":1974,\"country\":\"USA\",\"director\":\"Bob Clark\",\"imageUrl\":\"https://horrorcritic.com/wp-content/uploads/2012/10/Deathdream2.jpg\",\"imageCropX\":50,\"imageCropY\":50,\"ageCertificate\":\"18\"}}",
