          "redirectURL": "",
          "content": {
            "mimeType": "application/json",
            "text": "{\"id\":111,\"filmId\":91,\"screeningDate\":\"2026-02-11T20:45\",\"doorsTime\":\"8:15pm\",\"filmTime\":\"8:45\",\"capacity\":37,\"ticketsSold\":0,\"price\":1100,\"format\":\"Digital\",\"excludeFromGiftVouchers\":false,\"film\":{\"id\":91,\"title\":\"THE LIVING CORPSE\",\"description\":\"Often referred to as Pakistan’s first horror film, The Living Corpse (Zinda Laash) adapts the classic Dracula narrative into a uniquely South Asian context. A scientist’s experiments with life-and-death boundaries unleash a vampire-like figure on an isolated mansion and its environs, drawing in villagers and a determined local doctor as the night’s dread deepens. The creeping menace fuses gothic atmosphere with moments of unsettling surrealism, punctuated by strikingly incongruous song-and-dance interludes that reflect the region’s cinematic traditions.\\n\\nBlending horror conventions with expressive genre play, The Living Corpse stands as a singular early example of Pakistani horror cinema. Its homage to Western vampire films is filtered through a lens of regional performance styles and narrative rhythms, producing a work that is both an artifact of its time and a curious outlier in the global horror canon.\",\"runtime\":103,\"year\":1967,\"country\":\"Pakistan\",\"director\":\"Khwaja Sarfraz\",\"imageUrl\":\"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/M/MV5BYTRjZDAzNmMtYmZjYS00MzhkLTgzMTMtYWJlYjU4ZWE2ZDJlXkEyXkFqcGc@._V1_.jpg\",\"imageCropX\":50,\"imageCropY\":50,\"ageCertificate\":\"18\"}}",
