          "headersSize": 697,
          "cookies": [],
          "redirectURL": "",
          "content": {
            "mimeType": "application/json",
            "text": "{\"id\":114,\"filmId\":93,\"screeningDate\":\"2026-02-13T18:30\",\"doorsTime\":\"6pm\",\"filmTime\":\"6:30pm\",\"capacity\":37,\"ticketsSold\":1,\"price\":1100,\"format\":\"Digital\",\"excludeFromGiftVouchers\":false,\"film\":{\"id\":93,\"title\":\"CLIFFORD\",\"description\":\"Ten-year-old Clifford — played with deliberately unhinged, adult-inhabiting energy by Martin Short — is left in the care of his uncle (Charles Grodin), an architect whose carefully ordered life begins to unravel under the boy’s escalating mischief. What starts as a simple attempt to entertain a visiting relative spirals into a sequence of deranged schemes, emotional manipulations, and catastrophic set-pieces, all centred on Clifford’s obsessive desire to visit a dinosaur-themed amusement park.\\n\\nOperating at the intersection of family comedy and surreal behavioural satire, Clifford has gained its cult reputation through the sheer oddity of its central performance, where Short’s child persona becomes a destabilising force within an otherwise conventional domestic narrative. The result is a film that oscillates between absurdity and deadpan discomfort, revealing a comedic tone far stranger and more anarchic than its studio packaging suggests.\",\"runtime\":89,\"year\":1994,\"country\":\"USA\",\"director\":\"Paul Flaherty\",\"imageUrl\":\"https://pyxis.nymag.com/v1/imgs/f71/70f/bdd7eb154cc379925b9d1c76e628f954b5-clifford-oral-history-08.2x.rhorizontal.w700.jpg\",\"imageCropX\":50,\"imageCropY\":50,\"ageCertificate\":\"PG\"}}",
