          "headersSize": 697,
          "cookies": [],
          "redirectURL": "",
          "content": {
            "mimeType": "application/json",
            "text": "{\"id\":132,\"filmId\":107,\"screeningDate\":\"2026-02-21T21:00\",\"doorsTime\":\"8:30pm\",\"filmTime\":\"9pm\",\"capacity\":37,\"ticketsSold\":0,\"price\":1100,\"format\":\"Digital\",\"excludeFromGiftVouchers\":false,\"film\":{\"id\":107,\"title\":\"BONE\",\"description\":\"When a Beverly Hills couple — affluent in appearance but unraveling beneath the surface — discovers an intruder in their backyard, the encounter spirals into a tense, ambiguous standoff. Bone (Yaphet Kotto), the intruder, quickly recognises the fractures in their marriage and exploits them with a mix of menace, insight, and dark humour. What begins as a home-invasion scenario mutates into a psychological negotiation in which power shifts unpredictably, revealing the couple’s anxieties, prejudices, and long-suppressed resentments.\\n\\nOperating at the intersection of social satire, exploitation cinema, and chamber psychological drama, Bone dismantles the architecture of suburban respectability with Cohen’s characteristic sharpness. The film uses its small cast and contained setting to probe race, class, and the instability of American domestic life in the early 1970s, resulting in a work that is volatile, confrontational, and far more thematically layered than its premise initially suggests.\",\"runtime\":95,\"year\":1972,\"country\":\"USA\",\"director\":\"Larry Cohen\",\"imageUrl\":\"https://petersonreviews.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/screen-shot-2024-08-26-at-9.28.32-am.png?w=1024\",\"imageCropX\":50,\"imageCropY\":50,\"ageCertificate\":\"18\"}}",
