          "content": {
            "mimeType": "application/json",
            "text": "{\"id\":146,\"filmId\":119,\"screeningDate\":\"2026-02-27T22:30\",\"doorsTime\":\"8pm\",\"filmTime\":\"8:30pm\",\"capacity\":37,\"ticketsSold\":0,\"price\":1100,\"format\":\"Digital\",\"excludeFromGiftVouchers\":false,\"film\":{\"id\":119,\"title\":\"DOA: A RIGHT OF PASSAGE\",\"description\":\"Filmed across the late 1970s, D.O.A.: A Right of Passage documents the early international eruption of punk with an emphasis on the Sex Pistols’ chaotic and short-lived U.S. tour. Interspersed with candid interviews, club performances, and street-level encounters, the film captures a moment when punk existed as raw impulse rather than codified style: a collision of youthful antagonism, cultural anxiety, and institutional backlash. Alongside the Pistols, Kowalski’s camera turns to other emerging acts — including The Dead Boys and Generation X — as well as the fans and detractors who defined the scene’s volatile public face.\\n\\nKowalski’s approach is observational and unvarnished, favouring immediacy over narrative cohesion. The result is a documentary that functions less as a polished historical record than as a sociological snapshot of subcultural energy at breaking point — a portrait of a movement still forming its identity in real time, as commercial forces and moral panic gathered at its edges.\",\"runtime\":96,\"year\":1980,\"country\":null,\"director\":\"Lech Kowalski\",\"imageUrl\":\"https://optimise2.assets-servd.host/utter-seal/production/uploads/DOA6.jpg?w=1200&h=675&q=75&fm=jpg&fit=crop&dm=1581666136&s=3ad368b5821e5aeae244b33e11e87978\",\"imageCropX\":50,\"imageCropY\":50,\"ageCertificate\":\"18\"}}",
